[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Automation Testing Framework in Unreal Engine: Unit and Functional Tests"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unreal Engine features a robust built-in Automation Testing Framework designed to help developers write and execute both unit tests and functional tests. Implementing a solid testing pipeline ensures code stability, prevents regressions, and catches bugs early in the development cycle."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Unit Tests in Unreal Engine"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unit tests are designed to test small, isolated pieces of C++ logic without needing to spawn actors or enter a game world. They inherit from automation test macros and run extremely fast."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"#include "Misc/AutomationTest.h"

IMPLEMENT_SIMPLE_AUTOMATION_TEST(FMathUtilityTest, "MyGame.Unit.MathUtility", EAutomationTestFlags::EditorContext | EAutomationTestFlags::EngineFilter)

bool FMathUtilityTest::RunTest(const FString& Parameters)
{
    // Test a custom math function
    int32 Result = FMath::Square(4);
    TestEqual("Square of 4 should be 16", Result, 16);
    return true;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Understanding Functional Tests"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functional tests execute within a live map context. They allow you to test gameplay systems, actor interactions, AI behavior, and UI flows by spawning an AFunctionalTest actor directly into your test level."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Functional tests can be written in C++ by subclassing AFunctionalTest."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Alternatively, you can place a FunctionalTest Blueprint actor in the level and implement test logic via event graphs."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Writing a C++ Functional Test"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"#include "FunctionalTest.h"
#include "MyHealthComponent.h"
#include "FunctionalTestHealth.h"

bool AFunctionalTestHealth::IsTestReady_Implementation()
{
    return Super::IsTestReady_Implementation();
}

void AFunctionalTestHealth::StartTest()
{
    Super::StartTest();

    // Perform test logic
    UMyHealthComponent* HealthComp = NewObject<UMyHealthComponent>(this);
    HealthComp->InitializeHealth(100.0f);
    HealthComp->TakeDamage(30.0f);

    if (FMath::IsNearlyEqual(HealthComp->GetCurrentHealth(), 70.0f))
    {
        FinishTest(EFunctionalTestResult::Passed, TEXT("Health calculation is correct."));
    }
    else
    {
        FinishTest(EFunctionalTestResult::Failed, TEXT("Health calculation failed."));
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Running Tests via the Session Frontend"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"To execute your tests inside the Unreal Editor, navigate to Window -> Developer Tools -> Session Frontend. Select the Automation tab, locate your test hierarchy under categories like MyGame, check the boxes, and click Start Tests."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Executing Tests via Command Line (CI/CD Pipeline)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can automate your testing pipeline by running Unreal Engine via the command line. This is ideal for continuous integration systems like Jenkins or GitHub Actions."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"UE4Editor.exe "C:/Projects/MyGame/MyGame.uproject" -ExecCmds="Automation RunTests MyGame" -unattended -nopause -NullRHI"}]
